Agree on project ownership, workflow, identifier allocation, and transfer responsibilities before several people enter records.

{% bpdiagram kind="merge" locale="en" /%}

## Assign a single source of truth

**Designate one device to maintain the authoritative project copy.** That device holds the master taxon list, personnel, and custom-field definitions, and distributes updates to other devices, which do not edit those shared definitions.

Match how sites and events are shared to your [field workflow](/en/usages/workflows/):

- **Shared site, parallel catalogs (Workflow A).** The authoritative device creates the shared sites, coordinates, and events. Teammates scan the QR or import the JSON and add their own specimens without editing the shared records.
- **Separate sites, parallel catalogs (Workflow B).** The authoritative device shares project identity, personnel, and taxa. Each line leader creates sites and events for their own lines, using their initials in each `Site ID` (such as `GPHH-L1`), so records arrive cleanly at merge time.
- **Assembly line, one catalog (Workflow C).** One scribe enters every record on a single device and credits teammates in event personnel and as `Preparator` on the specimen parts they prepared.

**Handle every project merge on the authoritative device.** It reviews UUID conflicts and section-level choices before committing the combined project.

## Name and identify the project

**Use a short, recognizable name** (3–25 characters, such as `Patah Mammals`), record `Accession` and `Location`, and set `Time zone` to the fieldwork location's IANA time zone (such as `Asia/Jakarta`). NAHPU never converts dates or times across time zones — it stores the field's local clock exactly as entered, and the project time zone documents which clock that is.

A project UUID identifies the project for transfers; it is separate from `Accession`, the project's `Field ID` counter, and each specimen's readable Field ID. **Recreating a project with the same name does not recreate its UUID.**

Give sites stable IDs and keep their meaning in the locality description. See [Data Collection](/en/best-practices/data-collection/).

## Agree on Field ID conventions

**Choose the convention that matches your collection protocol.** Project Field IDs suit a shared processing sequence; personnel Field IDs suit work organized around catalogers' individual sequences.

**NAHPU does not automatically synchronize devices.** It is purposely designed that way so that every transfer and merge is deliberate and reviewed.

**Document handoffs and check duplicates after transfers.** UUIDs distinguish records internally, but they do not prevent duplicate readable Field IDs or mislabeled physical specimens.

{% aside type="tip" title="Deciding which Field ID to use" %}
If you are starting a lab, or your lab does not yet have a standard Field ID convention, in our experience **personnel Field IDs** work best for parallel specimen processing, where multiple people prepare specimens and record data on their own devices (Workflows A and B). They carry far less risk of ID duplication. A **project Field ID** is more appropriate when data is recorded on a single device in a single sequence (Workflow C), where each teammate has a distinct role such as collecting tissues, photographing, or preparing vouchers.
{% /aside %}

## Manage contributions {% #manage-contributions %}

**Establish a common project identity before distributing work.** Assign responsibility for shared personnel, taxa, vocabularies, and custom-field definitions. Agree when each device stops editing for a handoff and who reviews the combined project.

- **`Import project`** on the home screen brings a project onto a device that does not have it yet.
- **`Merge project`** inside an existing project combines contributions; review UUID conflicts and section-level choices (`Keep current`, `Use imported`, `Import as new`, or `Skip`). Nothing is written until the final step — NAHPU commits all changes together or leaves the database unchanged if an error occurs. Do not force a UUID mismatch merely because two projects share a similar name.
- **Back up before merging.** Use the `Back up now` button built into `Merge project`, and keep the source packages and pre-merge backup until the merged project has been checked.

**Record who supplied and reviewed each package, and what was decided at each handoff, while the team is still together.** Write a short entry in the project's `Narrative` so merge decisions travel with the data. A note written at camp is worth more than a reconstruction attempted next year.

{% aside type="note" title="Information entropy" %}
[Michener et al. (1997)](https://doi.org/10.1890/1051-0761%281997%29007%5B0330%3ANMFTES%5D2.0.CO%3B2) describe this as resisting information entropy: the interpretive content of a dataset degrades over time as the people who held it in memory disperse.
{% /aside %}

## Keep a backup routine

Agree on who runs daily project exports, weekly database backups, and off-device copies before leaving camp. See [Backups and Recovery](/en/best-practices/backups-and-recovery/) for the full cadence, naming conventions, and restore checks, and [Transfer a Project](/en/usages/export/project-transfer/) for step-by-step transfer details.

